Experiment No.: 2
Title

Demonstration of setup() and loop() Functions

Aim

To demonstrate the working of setup() and loop() functions in Arduino.

Theory

In Arduino programming, the setup() function is executed only once when the Arduino board starts or resets. It is used for initialization tasks such as setting pin modes and starting communication. The loop() function runs continuously after the setup() function and contains the main program instructions.

Components Required
Arduino Uno
USB Cable
Computer/Laptop
Procedure
Connect the Arduino Uno to the computer using a USB cable.
Open the Arduino IDE.
Select Arduino Uno board and appropriate COM port.
Write the program using setup() and loop() functions.
Compile the program to check for errors.
Upload the program to Arduino Uno.
Open the Serial Monitor.
Observe the output messages.
Code
void setup()
{
  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println("Setup executed once");
}

void loop()
{
  Serial.println("Loop running...");
  delay(1000);
}
Output

The Serial Monitor displays:

Setup executed once
Loop running...
Loop running...
Loop running...
Result

The setup() and loop() functions were successfully demonstrated.

Conclusion

The working of Arduino setup() and loop() functions was studied successfully. It was observed that setup() executes once, while loop() executes repeatedly.
